<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <key>Label</key>
  <string>com.buildandserve.projects-db-watch</string>

  <!-- One job, two checks, for the five project databases on the bas box.

       Certificate (LAC-6054): every project URL is sslmode=verify-full against
       a 90-day Let's Encrypt cert. A renewal that stops working is a
       simultaneous hard outage for every project, with no partial degradation
       to notice first.

       Authentication failures (LAC-6059): tcp/26432 is world-open for Vercel,
       so scram is the only thing between the internet and those databases.
       log_connections has been on since the box was built and nothing read it.

       Scheduled by launchd, not by a Paperclip routine: the box this watches
       also carries the BAS control plane, so a routine could be unable to run
       for the same reason the check would have fired.
       Runbook: ~/repo/buildandserve/docs/ops/bas-projects-db.md

       Runs an installed copy of the script under ~/.paperclip/scripts, not
       scripts/bas-projects-db-watch.sh in the repo. ~/repo/buildandserve is
       the shared working tree for every agent in the fleet and is routinely
       parked on a feature branch: when this job was first installed that
       clone sat on a brand branch 24 commits behind main, where this script
       does not exist, so a job pointing into the repo would have failed
       hourly with "No such file or directory" and a watch that cannot run is
       worse than no watch at all. "Install it once the branch is on main" is
       not enough of a guard, because the script can be on main and still be
       absent from the checkout. The installed copy can drift from main, so
       the runbook verifies it by md5, the same way it verifies the box's own
       copy of scripts/bas-projects-db.sh (LAC-6097). -->
  <key>ProgramArguments</key>
  <array>
    <string>/bin/bash</string>
    <string>/Users/lacy/.paperclip/scripts/bas-projects-db-watch.sh</string>
    <string>--notify</string>
  </array>

  <!-- Hourly, on the hour. An auth-failure threshold measured per hour and
       reported once a day is a threshold that tells you about last night.
       The cert check does not need this cadence and does not get it: the
       script keeps its own stamp file and runs that half at most once every
       20 hours, so the hourly run is one ssh and a log parse.

       StartCalendarInterval rather than StartInterval, because launchd runs a
       missed calendar job on wake and that is what makes this survive a lid
       that was shut overnight. -->
  <key>StartCalendarInterval</key>
  <dict>
    <key>Minute</key>
    <integer>0</integer>
  </dict>

  <!-- Silence means healthy: the script prints nothing and exits 0 when the
       cert is fine and nobody is guessing passwords, so a growing log file is
       itself a signal. Exit 3 means the run proved nothing (this host could
       not do DNS, or the tailnet was down) and is not an incident. -->
  <key>StandardOutPath</key>
  <string>/Users/lacy/.paperclip/logs/projects-db-watch.log</string>
  <key>StandardErrorPath</key>
  <string>/Users/lacy/.paperclip/logs/projects-db-watch.log</string>

  <key>RunAtLoad</key>
  <false/>
</dict>
</plist>
